{"success": true, "name": "arc-compact-panel-encoding", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md", "content": "# Session learning: lattice-like compact outputs

The test was a 24x28 repeating 3x3 panel field separated by color-9 bands, with distinctive colored values at selected separator intersections. Two demos confirmed that this general puzzle class can produce compact outputs, but neither established a test mapping: demo 1 was 23x29 -> 5x4, demo 2 was 26x29 -> 3x4, while the test was 24x28. Their compact outputs preserve some colors from the separator markings and include the filler/background value (9 in demo 1, 7 in demo 2). Dimensions do not match a simple count of all intersections or panels. The tested agent requested a third demo without stating a concrete pair of competing mappings or the predicted observation that would distinguish them; no verified output rule was obtained.

Reusable diagnostic: before requesting another example, lay out candidate coordinate maps and output conventions. For each demo, record exact positions/values of exceptional separator cells and compact output. Test possible selection of intersections, row/column reduction, edge inclusion, ordering, and background/default encoding against every observed pair. Make a further request only if the competing hypotheses yield a specific different predicted outcome, then immediately use the returned example to resolve that difference. Do not infer the test result from visual lattice resemblance or output shape alone.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-compact-panel-encoding/references/session-f0caf098-demo-learning.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/task-f0caf098-intersection-ledger.md", "content": "# Divider-crossing demos with varying compact outputs

This task family presented large regular lattices whose outputs were much smaller, with marked values placed on divider intersections. Across five examples, input/output dimensions varied substantially (23×29→5×4, 26×29→3×4, 22×30→1×1, 28×23→3×3, 29×14→5×4). The outputs echoed some values found at marked crossings, but the examples did not establish a direct full crossing-matrix extraction or a unique rule for edge inclusion, ordering, or default values. The 24×28 test's lattice appearance therefore did not justify a specific compact output.

Reusable response: maintain an explicit coordinate ledger of divider lines, exceptional intersections, and every output cell; compare candidate direct, cropped/selected, and panel-summary mappings against all demos. Before another request, state two competing mappings and a decision-relevant predicted observation. If no concrete discriminator exists, do not chain requests or submit a guess based only on visual resemblance. This is unresolved evidence, not a solved transformation.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/task-f0caf098-intersection-ledger.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md", "content": "# Compact output representation and task-f0caf098 review

The demos repeatedly presented large regular lattices but returned compact outputs whose dimensions varied substantially. A key caution is that identifying separator crossings or extracting marker colors is only a candidate representation: neither the test's lattice dimensions nor a plausible crossing matrix establishes the output shape/content. Across demonstrations, explicitly align region/intersection positions to each compact output and test the precise encoding (including blank/default cells, omitted borders, ordering, and exceptional marker values). Require exact replay of every demo before submission.

In the reviewed instance, I made successive unsupported compact-grid guesses (including 4x4 and 5x5 crossing matrices) despite failed feedback. After any incorrect compact-output submission, restart representation inference from the demos and record which assumption each failure rejects; do not repeat the same candidate or cosmetically alter its entries. The available skill references already flag this output-dimension/mapping issue; this note captures the concrete failure pattern and emphasizes full representation validation before transfer.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-f0caf098-output-representation.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-ledger", "file": "references/session-f0caf098-request-and-inference-review.md", "content": "# Demo integration and inference review

In this turn, the solver requested three demonstrations consecutively without first integrating each returned pair, despite the compact-grid task's representation being unresolved. The three input/output pairs varied in dimensions, palettes, and compact output shapes (23×23→5×6, 26×22→4×3, 27×29→6×4). The test itself has a 14×28 lattice with two horizontal and three vertical panel intervals; separator intersections include color 1 in the upper-left and color 4 across the right-side intersections. No common exact mapping was established from the three demos.

A speculative 4×4 grid of repeated marker colors was then submitted and rejected. This falsifies that candidate in full; do not tweak its colors or dimensions without new evidence. The key process correction is to stop after every demo and build an explicit ledger before another action: input/output dimensions, separator/region counts, intersection values, compact output cells, and candidate correspondence. A demo request must be driven by named competing mappings and a specific predicted distinction that would change the test answer. Similar-looking partitioned grids and task IDs are not validation; exact replay across all demos is required before submission.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/H-hermes-luna6-low-ws0/frozen/seen-3/play/users/u066/system/state/hermes/skills/puzzle-solving/arc-demo-ledger/references/session-f0caf098-request-and-inference-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-delta-discipline", "file": "references/speculative-submission-after-demo.md", "content": "# Avoiding speculative submission after a demo

A returned demonstration pair is evidence to analyze, not permission to extrapolate from a salient resemblance. Record the exact changed and unchanged cells, then check whether the rule is uniquely supported for the current test. If an assumed stamp, anchor, orientation, or source-retention rule is not established by the pair, do not submit a guessed grid. Name at least two plausible hypotheses and request another pair only if their predictions differ in a way that can change the test answer. Analyze every newly returned pair before acting again. This is especially important after a failed submission: changing only one guessed coordinate or preserving a different portion of the candidate is not meaningful recovery unless new evidence supports it.
